## Where it happens

`createTasks` in `src/domain/tasks.ts` (lines 294‑337), specifically the dependency-wiring pass at line 326‑331:

```ts
specs.forEach((spec, i) => {
  for (const ref of spec.depends_on ?? []) {
    const dependsOnTaskId = tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref;
    addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId);
  }
});
```

## Why it fires without a literal repeated id

Task creation (`createTask`, line 134) assigns each new task a fresh `randomUUID()` — the client never knows a spec's real id in advance, so it's true that no spec's `depends_on` ever contains its own eventual real id.

But the resolution logic treats a `depends_on` entry as a lookup key into `tempIdToTaskId`, which maps each spec's client-chosen `temp_id` to the real id that spec was just given. If a spec's own `depends_on` list contains **that same spec's own `temp_id`**, the lookup resolves right back to `results[i].task_id` — the id of the very task being wired. `addDependency` is then called with identical `taskId` and `dependsOnTaskId`, tripping the `taskId === dependsOnTaskId` check.

Nothing in `createTasks` guards against this: the only pre-check (lines 300‑308) rejects *duplicate* `temp_id`s across specs, not a spec referencing its own `temp_id` inside its own `depends_on`.

## Exact trigger shape

A single spec whose `depends_on` array includes its own `temp_id`:

```json
{
  "specs": [
    { "temp_id": "A", "title": "Build the widget", "depends_on": ["A"] }
  ]
}
```

At line 328, `ref` is `"A"`, `tempIdToTaskId.get("A")` returns the UUID just assigned to that same spec, so `dependsOnTaskId === results[i].task_id`, and `addDependency` throws "A task cannot depend on itself" — even though the client's raw JSON never mentions any real task id at all, let alone repeats it.

The self-dependency error surfaces inside `createTasks` in `src/domain/tasks.ts` (lines 294–337), specifically at the second pass loop (lines 326–331) that resolves `depends_on` references and calls `addDependency`.

**Where it happens:**

```ts
for (const spec of specs) {              // pass 1: create every task first
  ...
  if (temp_id) tempIdToTaskId.set(temp_id, task.id);
  ...
}

specs.forEach((spec, i) => {              // pass 2: wire up edges
  for (const ref of spec.depends_on ?? []) {
    const dependsOnTaskId = tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref;
    addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId);
  }
});
```

Because task creation (pass 1) runs to completion *before* any dependency is resolved (pass 2), the `tempIdToTaskId` map is fully populated — including each spec's own `temp_id` mapping to its own freshly-created real `task_id` — before pass 2 even starts.

**The triggering input shape:**

A spec in the batch that lists **its own `temp_id`** inside its own `depends_on` array, e.g.:

```json
{
  "temp_id": "impl-login",
  "title": "Implement login",
  "depends_on": ["impl-login"]
}
```

The client never writes a real task id anywhere — it only references its own symbolic `temp_id`, which is legal-looking (it's meant to reference *other* siblings created in the same batch). But by the time pass 2 runs, `tempIdToTaskId.get("impl-login")` has already been populated with that very spec's own new `task_id`. So `dependsOnTaskId` resolves to `results[i].task_id`, which is identical to `taskId` being passed as the first argument — and `addDependency` throws `"A task cannot depend on itself"` even though nothing in the client's JSON payload literally repeats a real id anywhere.

This is distinct from the trivial case (a spec depending on an *already-existing* task id equal to itself, which can't happen since the id doesn't exist yet) — the bug is purely a consequence of resolving temp-id self-references against a map that's already complete for the whole batch, including the referencing spec itself.

Here's what I found and how I'd build it.

## What's already there

**`cancelTask`** (`src/domain/lifecycle.ts:425`) is the single-task primitive. Inside one `db.transaction`, it:
- 404s on an unknown `task_id` (plain `Error`)
- rejects with a **plain `Error`** (not a typed class) if the task is already `done`/`cancelled` — `"Task X is already Y, nothing to cancel"`
- rejects with `TaskConflictError` if `owner_session_id` is set and the lock hasn't expired (`isLockActive`), *unless* `force: true` — note there's no `session_id` param here (unlike claim/release), so it's owner-agnostic: any active lock blocks a plain call
- on success: sets `status='cancelled'`, `cancellation_reason`, clears `owner_session_id`/`lock_expires_at`, and returns `unblocked_task_ids` via `findUnblockedDependents` (`status.ts:91`)

**Getting an epic's tasks**: `requireEpic(db, projectId, ref)` (`src/domain/epics.ts:67`) resolves by id or name and 404s otherwise. `epicTaskCounts` (`status.ts:254`) shows the query shape already used elsewhere: `SELECT id, status FROM tasks WHERE epic_id = ?`.

## The design

The key move is to **not** loop over every task and catch "already done" errors — instead filter them out of the candidate set up front with SQL, since that's literally what "isn't already done or cancelled" means:

```sql
SELECT id FROM tasks WHERE epic_id = ? AND status NOT IN ('done', 'cancelled')
```

That structurally eliminates the "crashes on the first finished task" failure mode — `cancelTask` is never even called on one.

The remaining risk is the **active-lock case** (someone else's in-progress task). A naive `for (id of candidates) cancelTask(...)` loop would throw on the first `TaskConflictError` and abort the whole batch, leaving the rest of the epic half-cancelled with no clear record of why. So each call needs its own try/catch, and conflicts get collected rather than propagated — the batch keeps going and reports which tasks it couldn't touch instead of dying partway through.

```ts
export interface BulkCancelEpicTasksInput {
  project_id: string;
  epic_id: string;
  reason?: string;
  force?: boolean; // same semantics as cancelTask's force: overrides active locks too
}

export interface BulkCancelEpicTasksResult {
  cancelled_task_ids: string[];
  skipped_conflicts: Array<{
    task_id: string;
    current_owner_session_id: string | null;
    lock_expires_at: string | null;
  }>;
  unblocked_task_ids: string[];
}

export function bulkCancelEpicTasks(
  db: DatabaseInstance,
  input: BulkCancelEpicTasksInput,
): BulkCancelEpicTasksResult {
  const project = requireProject(db, input.project_id);
  const epic = requireEpic(db, project.id, input.epic_id);

  const candidates = db
    .prepare(`SELECT id FROM tasks WHERE epic_id = ? AND status NOT IN ('done', 'cancelled')`)
    .all(epic.id) as Array<{ id: string }>;

  const cancelledTaskIds: string[] = [];
  const skippedConflicts: BulkCancelEpicTasksResult["skipped_conflicts"] = [];
  const unblocked = new Set<string>();

  for (const { id } of candidates) {
    try {
      const result = cancelTask(db, { task_id: id, reason: input.reason, force: input.force });
      cancelledTaskIds.push(id);
      result.unblocked_task_ids.forEach((u) => unblocked.add(u));
    } catch (error) {
      if (error instanceof TaskConflictError) {
        skippedConflicts.push({
          task_id: id,
          current_owner_session_id: error.currentOwnerSessionId,
          lock_expires_at: error.lockExpiresAt,
        });
        continue;
      }
      throw error; // e.g. unknown task_id — shouldn't happen given the pre-filter, so surface it rather than swallowing
    }
  }

  return { cancelled_task_ids: cancelledTaskIds, skipped_conflicts: skippedConflicts, unblocked_task_ids: [...unblocked] };
}
```

Other details that matter:
- **Per-task transactions, not one big one.** `cancelTask` already wraps each cancellation in its own `db.transaction`; deliberately *don't* wrap the whole loop in an outer transaction, or one conflict/error would roll back every task already cancelled in that batch.
- **Dedup `unblocked_task_ids`** with a `Set` — cancelling several sibling tasks in the same epic can unblock the same dependent more than once.
- **Stale-read safety**: the candidate list is read once up front, but that's fine — `cancelTask` re-reads each task's live status/lock inside its own transaction, so a task that changed state between the query and its turn in the loop is handled correctly (e.g. it'll surface as a conflict, not silently misfire).
- **No epic-level status to set.** Epics have no `status` column (`src/domain/epics.ts`) — this op only ever touches `tasks`, mirroring how `cancelRelease` deliberately leaves task membership alone (see the comment at `lifecycle.ts:379`); there's just nothing analogous to flip on the epic itself.
- **`force`** passed through 1:1 to each `cancelTask` call, so bulk-close-out can either respect active locks (default, reports them in `skipped_conflicts`) or steamroll them, same as the single-task tool.

Wiring the MCP tool would follow the existing pattern in `src/mcp/tools/lifecycle.ts` (`registerLifecycleTools`) — `jsonResult({ ok: true, ...result })`, no special error-result mapping needed since conflicts are now data in the response rather than a thrown error.

